+# CommitLore: Reaching All Users
+
+## The Problem
+
+Half the team uses editors and agents without pre-tool-use hooks. The current hook-based system (`onPreToolUse`) doesn't reach them, leaving half the team with no context about recorded decisions.
+
+Constraints:
+- Can't add hooks to tools we don't control (no plugin API)
+- Can't use checked-in repo-wide context (goes stale, no path signal)
+- Can't concatenate all records into a session preamble (loses path scoping)
+
+## Solution: Dual Path Injection
+
+**For hook-enabled users:**
+- Path-scoped records via `onPreToolUse` (existing hook system)
+- Records arrive just before the tool is called on that file
+- Minimal latency, perfect scope
+
+**For hook-less users:**
+- Path-scoped records via explicit injection points
+- Two mechanisms:
+
+### 1. Path-Aware Context (`contextForPath`)
+Called when an agent is instantiated with knowledge of a specific file:
+```ts
+const context = contextForPath(repo, filePath)
+// Returns formatted records for that file
+```
+Reaches users who invoke agents with a file path argument. Maintains scope because records are loaded just for the files being worked on.
+
+### 2. Session Context (`sessionContext`)
+Called once per session when agents are initialized:
+```ts
+const context = sessionContext(repo)
+// Returns all records (project-level decisions)
+```
+Reaches users even if agents don't have a specific file path. Trades path scoping for universality — this is why it's only called at setup time, not on every tool use.
+
+## Why This Works
+
+- **Reaches all users**: Doesn't depend on editor hooks or tool plugins
+- **Maintains scope**: Records are still tied to files/paths when possible
+- **Fallback chain**: Try path-aware first, fall back to session context
+- **No stale files**: Records come from git metadata, not checked-in files
+- **Minimal latency**: Context injection happens once (at setup) not per-tool
+
+## Tradeoff
+
+Session context loses path scoping (all records appear upfront), but only for users who lack path information. Hook-enabled and path-aware users get perfect scoping. This is acceptable because:
+- Session context is the fallback, not the primary path
+- It still avoids the "stale repository dump" problem (comes from git, not a file)
+- Users who need fine-grained scoping can use hook-based editors
